# Class: obo:HMDM_0000493 (Glycoside hydrolase family 2)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref "PMID:26090361") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:HMDM_0000493 "Glycoside hydrolases (GHs) (EC 3.2.1.X) hydrolyze glycosidic bonds between carbohydrates or between carbohydrate and non-carbohydrate components, for instance alcohols or phenols. To date, enzyme classification is based on amino acid similarities rather than substrate specificity. GHs are assigned to different groups based on conserved domains, and the families are numbered from 1 to 131 that are listed in the Carbohydrate-Active Enzymes Database. Glycoside hydrolases classified as GH2 were described to function as beta-galactosidase (EC 3.2.1.23) ; beta-mannosidase (EC 3.2.1.25); beta-glucuronidase (EC 3.2.1.31); alpha-L-arabinofuranosidase (EC 3.2.1.55); mannosylglycoprotein endo-beta-mannosidase (EC 3.2.1.152); exo-beta-glucosaminidase (EC 3.2.1.165); alpha-L-arabinopyranosidase (EC 3.2.1.-); beta-galacturonidase (EC 3.2.1.-); beta-xylosidase (EC 3.2.1.37); beta-D-galactofuranosidase (EC 3.2.1.146); beta-glucosidase (EC 3.2.1.21); beta-galacturonidase RGII specific (EC 3.2.1.-); glycyrrhizin beta-glucuronidase (EC 3.2.1.128). These enzymes hydrolyze their substrates through a retaining acid/base mechanism with two glutamic acid/glutamate residues involved. One residue acts as acid and base catalyst, whereas the other residue serves as nucleophile.")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:contributor obo:HMDM_0000493 <https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace obo:HMDM_0000493 "human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:id obo:HMDM_0000493 "HMDM:0000493")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:HMDM_0000493 "Glycoside hydrolase family 2")
SubClassOf(obo:HMDM_0000493 obo:HMDM_0000012)

# Class: obo:HMDM_0000494 (Endoglucanase E precursor (EGE))

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref "PMID:1366808") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref "PMID:29856054") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:HMDM_0000494 "There are many subtypes of endoglucanases (e.g., endoglucanase I, II) each with its own molecular topology with some organisms capable of producing several different subtypes of endoglucanases. As a whole these endoglucanases are grouped into glycoside hydrolase families (glycoside hydrolase 5, 6, etc.). Endoglucanases vary in their substrate specificity being able to cleave differing glycosidic bonds (beta-1-4, beta-1-6, etc.).The Endoglucanase E (EGE) enzyme has a wide substrate specificity and cleaves beta1,4- and beta1,3- glycosidic linkages in addition to exhibiting activity on beta1,4- xylosidic bonds. The primary structure of EGE also has several unusual and notable features. BAL31 studies revealed that 300 C-terminal residues including those of the conserved region can be deleted from the enzyme without loss of catalytic activity. An endoglucanase E precursor would be a physiologically inactive substance that could at one point through a process be converted into endoglucanase E precursor.")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:contributor obo:HMDM_0000494 <https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace obo:HMDM_0000494 "human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:id obo:HMDM_0000494 "HMDM:0000494")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:HMDM_0000494 "Endoglucanase E precursor (EGE)")
SubClassOf(obo:HMDM_0000494 obo:HMDM_0000012)

