# Class: obo:HMDM_0000843 (PEMT (Phospholipid methyltransferase) super family)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref "PMID:19366698") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:HMDM_0000843 "Phospholipid N-methyltransferase (PLMT) enzymes catalyze the S-adenosylmethionine-dependent methylation of ethanolamine-containing phospholipids to produce phosphatidylcholine. Phosphatidylcholine is the most abundant phospholipid in most non-plastid membranes of eukaryotes and functions as a structural membrane lipid, and as a precursor to important lipid-based signaling molecules. In mammals and fungi, phosphatidylcholine can be formed through two distinct pathways: (a) the “nucleotide pathway” in which free choline is incorporated in phosphatidylcholine using CDP-choline as an intermediate, and (b) the “methylation pathway” whereby phosphatidylcholine is produced directly from phosphatidylethanolamine (PtdEtn) via three sequential methylation reactions using S-adenosylmethionine (AdoMet) as the methyl donor. In plants, phosphatidylcholine biosynthesis occurs through a branched pathway that utilizes components of both the nucleotide and methylation pathways. In mammals, the 18-kDa integral membrane protein phosphatidylethanolamine N-methyltransferase (PEMT) is a PLMT that is expressed primarily in the liver. PEMT catalyzes all three of the methylation reactions needed to convert PtdEtn to phosphatidylcholine.")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:contributor obo:HMDM_0000843 <https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace obo:HMDM_0000843 "human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:id obo:HMDM_0000843 "HMDM:0000843")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:HMDM_0000843 "PEMT (Phospholipid methyltransferase) super family")
