AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:HMDM_0000847 "oxidation of a molecule")
SubClassOf(obo:HMDM_0000847 obo:HMDM_0000015)

# Class: obo:HMDM_0000848 (catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) inhibitor)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref "PMID:23713800") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:HMDM_0000848 "Catechol O-methyltransferase catalyzes the methylation reaction whereby a methyl group from the cofactor S-adenosyl-L-methionine (SAM) is transferred to one of the catecholic hydroxyls. Membrane-bound form COMT (MB-COMT) may be more relevant in inactivation of catecholaminergic neurotransmitters, whereas the soluble form (S-COMT) plays a more important role in inactivation of endogenous and xenobiotic catechols in other tissues. COMT is the primary enzyme that inactivates the catechol neurotransmitter dopamine and the drug L-dopa. L-dopa is used in the clinical treatment of central nervous system (CNS) disorders such as Parkinson's disease and possibly others (e.g. schizophrenia and depression). Its efficacy is associated with the level of dopamine converted from the drug. Studies have shown that inhibition of COMT activity results in marked reduction of the body clearance of L-dopa and dopamine, leading to a sustained level of dopamine in the brain and improved efficacy. The important role of COMT in treatment of Parkinson's disease has promoted an area wherein the aims are to design potent and selective COMT inhibitors. Several of these inhibitors have been used as adjuncts to L-dopa therapy.")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:contributor obo:HMDM_0000848 <https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace obo:HMDM_0000848 "human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:id obo:HMDM_0000848 "HMDM:0000848")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:HMDM_0000848 "catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) inhibitor")
SubClassOf(obo:HMDM_0000848 obo:HMDM_0000073)

# Class: obo:HMDM_0000881 (histamine (H2-receptor) antagonist)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref "PMID:33206207") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:HMDM_0000881 "Histamine is primarily found in mast cells and basophils, where it is stored in cytoplasmic granules and released along with other inflammatory mediators upon activation in response to diverse immune and non-immune stimuli, including viruses and other pathogens. Histamine exerts multiple (patho)physiological actions by activating four known types of histamine receptors that are designated as H1–H4 and have been associated with allergic inflammation, stimulation of gastric acid secretion, neurotransmission and immune responses, respectively. This led to drugs, such as H1 antihistamines for the management of allergies, H2 receptor antagonists for the treatment of gastrointestinal disorders, a first-in-class H3 receptor antagonist for treating narcolepsy, as well as H4 receptor-targeting compounds that are being evaluated in clinical trials for their potential exploitation in managing inflammatory disorders.")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:contributor obo:HMDM_0000881 <https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace obo:HMDM_0000881 "human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:id obo:HMDM_0000881 "HMDM:0000881")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:HMDM_0000881 "histamine (H2-receptor) antagonist")
SubClassOf(obo:HMDM_0000881 obo:HMDM_0000073)

# Class: obo:HMDM_0000887 (Crp/Fnr family transcriptional regulator)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref "PMID:34636676") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:HMDM_0000887 "One particular transcription factors (TF), the fumarate and nitrate reductase regulatory protein (FNR), is among the most widely studied global regulators, activating and repressing transcription of a wide variety of functional genes. FNR is widely conserved across bacteria and is often required for the shift between aerobic and anaerobic metabolism. FNR belongs to the catabolite repressor protein (CRP; also known as cAMP receptor protein)/FNR family of TFs, which plays an important role across bacteria and generally shares a conserved DNA binding site. Like FNR, CRP is well studied and widely conserved across bacteria and responds to variations in glucose availability via changes in the concentration of cyclic AMP. Besides CRP and FNR, the CRP/FNR family of proteins contains other well-studied TFs that respond to a variety of environmental stimuli.")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:contributor obo:HMDM_0000887 <https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace obo:HMDM_0000887 "human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:id obo:HMDM_0000887 "HMDM:0000887")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:HMDM_0000887 "Crp/Fnr family transcriptional regulator")
SubClassOf(obo:HMDM_0000887 obo:HMDM_0000012)

